      "title": "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/id": "fc04dbd9-9575-576c-beb0-4c05c8f66b17",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{4}-{e}^{-1-4x}.$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y={e}^{-1-4x}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-1-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-1-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}-{e}^{-1-4x}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-(-4{e}^{-1-4x}) \\\\  & =4({x}^{3}+{e}^{-1-4x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
